            "response_snippet": "I can guide you through the process of analyzing a PDF file using a Python library. We'll use PyPDF2 for reading PDF files and Chardet to detect potential issues with the PDF content.\n\nPlease note that not all \"unsafe\" code in PDFs is necessarily malicious. Some might be benign, while others could indicate vulnerabilities or security risks.\n\nFirst, let's install the necessary libraries:\n\n```bash\npip install PyPDF2 chardet pdfminer.six\n```\n\nNow, we'll write a Python script to analyze the file:\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom PyPDF2 import PdfReader\nimport chardet\nimport pdfminer.high_quality_layout\n\ndef get_system_configuration_keys():\n    # This is just an example. In practice, you'd want to use a more reliable method.\n    system_config = {\n        'os': 'Windows',\n        'python_version': '3.x'\n    }\n    return system_config\n\ndef analyze_pdf(file_path):\n    system_config = get_system_configuration_keys()\n\n    print(\"System configuration:\")\n    for key, value in system_config.items():\n        print(f\"{key}: {value}\")\n\n    try:\n        with open(file_path, 'rb') as file:\n            content = file.read()\n            result = chardet.detect(content)\n            encoding_name = result['encoding']\n\n            print(\"\\nPDF metadata:\")\n            print(f\"Title: {PdfReader(file).title}\")\n            print(f\"Author: {PdfReader(file).author}\")\n\n            layout = pdfminer.high_quality_layout.extract_layout(PdfReader(file))\n            print(\"Layout:\")\n            for page in layout:\n                for item in page['items']:\n                    if 'text' in item:\n                        print(item['text'])\n\n            print(\"\\nContent encoding:\", encoding_name)\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        print(f\"File not found: {file_path}\")\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"An error occurred: {e}\")\n\n# Replace 'report.pdf' with the path to your file\nanalyze_pdf('report.pdf')\n```\n\nTo enable debug mode and verbose flags, we can use Python's built-in `logging` module. Here's",
