            "response_snippet": "**Critical Exception Error Analysis**\n\nThe error message indicates a critical exception occurred during tool execution. This typically implies that there is an issue with the code or configuration that requires immediate attention from the development team.\n\n**Symptoms of Critical Exception:**\n\n1. **System Prompt Output**: The system prompt (usually `>`) was output to the user, indicating a failure in the normal execution flow.\n2. **Developer Escalation Required**: To recover from this error, developer escalation is required, implying that there may be underlying issues with the code or dependencies.\n\n**Potential Causes:**\n\n1. **Incorrect Configuration**: A misconfigured environment variable, setting, or property might have caused the critical exception.\n2. **Dependency Issues**: Incompatible or missing dependencies could lead to a failure in execution.\n3. **Code Issues**: Syntax errors, logical errors, or bugs within the code may be responsible for this error.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n1. **Review Configuration**: Double-check all configuration settings and environment variables that might be causing the issue.\n2. **Check Dependencies**: Verify that all dependencies are up-to-date and compatible with the current version of the tool.\n3. **Debug Code**: Review the code for any syntax or logical errors that may be contributing to the error.\n\n**Next Steps:**\n\n1. **Document Error Details**: Record the specific details of the error, including any system prompt output, error messages, and relevant logs.\n2. **Collaborate with Team**: Share the error details with the development team to discuss potential causes and solutions.\n3. **Apply Patches or Updates**: Apply any necessary patches or updates to resolve the issue.\n\nBy following these steps, you should be able to identify and address the root cause of the critical exception and restore normal execution flow.",
