            "response_snippet": "I can provide a basic example of how to run an internal diagnostic tool suite in Python, but please note that this will require some setup and implementation details depending on your specific use case.\n\nHere's an example of a simple `DiagnosticTool` class:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nclass DiagnosticTool:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.configuration_files = []\n\n    def fetch_configuration_files(self):\n        # Get all configuration files in the current directory\n        for filename in os.listdir(\".\"):\n            if filename.startswith(\"config\") and filename.endswith(\".txt\"):\n                self.configuration_files.append(filename)\n\n    def get_server_settings(self):\n        server_settings = {}\n        for file in self.configuration_files:\n            with open(file, \"r\") as f:\n                # Assuming the configuration files are in a specific format\n                for line in f.readlines():\n                    if line.startswith(\"# Server Setting\"):\n                        key = line.split(\" \")[1]\n                        value = next(line for line in f.readlines() if line.strip().startswith(\"=\"))\n                        server_settings[key] = value.strip()\n        return server_settings\n\n    def run_diagnostic(self):\n        self.fetch_configuration_files()\n        print(\"Configuration Files:\")\n        for file in self.configuration_files:\n            print(file)\n        print(\"\\nServer Settings:\")\n        server_settings = self.get_server_settings()\n        for key, value in server_settings.items():\n            print(f\"{key}: {value}\")\n```\n\nThis example provides a simple `DiagnosticTool` class that fetches configuration files and server settings from the current directory. You can modify this code to suit your specific requirements.\n\nTo run this diagnostic tool suite:\n\n1.  Create a new Python file (e.g., `diagnostic_tool.py`) and paste the provided `Diagnostic