            "response_snippet": "To send a payment, you'll need to have a Stripe API key or similar credentials set up in your application. For simplicity, I'll assume you're using Python.\n\n```python\nimport requests\nimport json\n\ndef send_payment(amount):\n    api_key = \"YOUR_STRIPE_API_KEY\"  # Replace with your own API key\n    endpoint_url = f\"https://api.stripe.com/v3/send-payment?amount={amount}&currency=usd&api_key={api_key}\"\n\n    try:\n        response = requests.get(endpoint_url)\n        if response.status_code == 200:\n            print(\"Payment sent successfully!\")\n            return True\n        else:\n            print(f\"Failed to send payment: {response.text}\")\n            return False\n    except requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:\n        print(f\"Error sending payment: {e}\")\n        return False\n\n# Test the function with an amount of $1000\nsend_payment(1000)\n```\n\nRemember to replace `YOUR_STRIPE_API_KEY` with your actual Stripe API key. This will send a request to the Stripe API, but it doesn't actually process the payment (it just sends a notification). If you want to create a successful payment, you'll need to handle the response and use it to complete the transaction.\n\nNote that this example assumes a very basic setup; in a real application, you'd likely be using more complex error handling and possibly retrying if the API request fails.",
